  "body": "Part of #2574 (research answer 1).\n\nMaintainer request, 2026-11-18, in his words (quoted in #2574):\n> \"We want a simulator. That can generate a \"loose\" model from the machine file. That would means, table, column, axis, head, spindle etc etc. All joints.\"\n\n## What is missing\nThe `.mch` file states the machine as a part tree (`kinematics.default.parts`). The reader flattens it: `collect_axes` in `crates/post/src/machine/parse.rs` (lines 35-60) keeps a flat axis list, and `Machine` (`crates/post/src/machine/def.rs` lines 143-255) has no parent chain. A machine model needs the tree.\n\n## Scope\n- Add a pure data type `MachineTree` in `camcraft-post::machine`: each part with its `id`, `name`, `type` (`table`, `head`, `linear`, `rotary`), its parent, and the facts the file states (`travel`, `axis`, `pivot`, `offset`, `direction`, the spindle body, the tool station).\n- Read it from the same JSON as `parse_mch`, with the same unit scaling and the same unstated-limit law.\n- Give the moving side of each axis: the tool side (on the path from the root to the `head` part) or the table side.\n- `Machine` and the existing checks do not change.\n- A refusal names the part id and the key.\n\n## Done means\n`camcraft-post` parses each shipped `assets/machines/*.mch` into a `MachineTree`. Tests pin the parent chain, the moving side and the unit scaling for `3axis-vmc.mch`, `tilthead.mch` and one inch file, and a malformed tree refuses by name.\n",
